  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nAs of noon, MSP has set a new daily record high of 64. We'll see if \nwe can get another degree or two of warmth out of the strong WAA \nover the next couple of hours, but encroaching stratus will limit \nany additional solar help. Speaking of the stratus, some reports of \ndrizzle/mist have popped up over the past hour or so across west \ncentral Minnesota. Went ahead and adjusted PoPs to account for this. \nBreezy south winds ahead of the cold front and deepening \nsurface low over the Dakotas are gusting 35-40 MPH across much \nof southern Minnesota. These should trend down through late this\nafternoon into the evening, so the Wind Advisory for Martin \nCounty looks to be in good shape with no plans to extend beyond \n23z. With the warm front continuing to move into northern \nMinnesota through this evening, some warm advective precip is \npossible this evening and overnight for portions of central \nMinnesota.\n\nThe cold front will track across Minnesota through early tomorrow \nmorning, with areas in western Minnesota likely seeing their high \ntemperatures for the day occur at midnight. Scattered showers \nduring the morning are possible, but the main round of rain is \nstill anticipated tomorrow afternoon. This will primarily fall \nacross southern/eastern Minnesota and western Wisconsin along \nthe cold front. Here, QPF amounts of around 0.25\" up to 0.5\" \nwill be possible. The axis of heaviest precip (greater than \n0.5\") still looks to set up to the southeast across WFO La \nCrosse's area. Temperatures will fall through the day with lows \novernight dipping below freezing for the first time in several \ndays for many. CAA will continue to work in on the backside of \nthe low through Sunday, setting the stage for a switch to below \nnormal temperatures to start next week. Canadian high pressure \nwill be centered over Minnesota to start the day Tuesday. \nCurrent forecast has lows in the teens for all (including the \nTwin Cities metro!) Tuesday morning. Looking ahead to the \nThanksgiving holiday, the forecast remains largely dry with \ntemperatures slowly warming back into the upper 30s to low 40s. \nModel agreement is poor beyond this time period, but overall, no\nmajor systems appear to be lurking.",
